INFO:agent.loop:\u26a1 Executing tool: 'knowledge_agent' with arguments: {"source_title": "Zero-Day Vulnerability in MOVEit Transfer Exploited for Data Theft", "source_url": "https://www.cisa.gov/news-events/cybersecurity-advisories/aa23-158a", "text": "SRC-001 reports Mandiant's June 2, 2023 analysis, updated June 9. Progress announced the MOVEit Transfer vulnerability on May 31, 2023 and it was assigned CVE-2023-34362. Earliest observed exploitation was May 27, 2023, involving web-shell deployment and data theft; in some cases theft occurred within minutes. The vulnerability was a SQL injection in MOVEit Transfer. Actors deployed a newly discovered C# web shell named LEMURLOOT, often using filenames human.aspx, human2.aspx, or _human2.aspx to masquerade as a legitimate component. POST requests to guestaccess.aspx preceded web-shell interaction, indicating SQL injection directed at that file. LEMURLOOT could enumerate files/folders, retrieve configuration and database information, download files, access Azure Blob storage settings/credentials, and create/delete a user named Health Check Service. Scanning/exploitation often came from IP range 5.252.188.0/22; second-stage interaction/data theft came from different systems. Many supporting hosts exposed RDP with certificates generated May 19-22, suggesting infrastructure staging. Mandiant initially attributed activity to UNC4857, then merged UNC4857 into FIN11 based on targeting, infrastructure, certificate, and data leak site overlaps. On June 6, 2023 the CL0P^_-LEAKS data leak site claimed responsibility and threatened publication absent payment; Mandiant says the activity was reminiscent of prior FIN11-attributed data-theft extortion via that site. Mandiant had not directly observed extortion emails to confirmed victims. Mandiant also noted a June 5 report that someone affiliated with CLOP claimed involvement. Mandiant described impacts in Canada, India, and the United States and said the broader impact was almost certainly larger. It identified overlaps in ISPs, netblocks, and IP addresses with historical FIN11 operations, including an exploitation IP previously used by FIN11 in January 2023 and retaining the same RDP certificate. These are Mandiant assessments/observations, not proof of operator identity."}
